{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was a nobleman from the Crusader states in the Levant He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140 He was the eldest of three sons of Raymond II and Hodierna He had two younger brothers Bohemond III and Bertha In 1152 Raymond II was killed during the Siege of Shaizar and Raymond III succeeded him as the count of Tripoli\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by conflicts with the nearby Muslim states and with the Byzantine Empire He also had to deal with internal disputes within the Crusader states He was involved in the succession crisis in the Kingdom of Jerusalem following the death of King Baldwin III in 1163 He supported the claim of his cousin, Princess Maria Joscelin of Jerusalem, to the throne but was ultimately unsuccessful\n\nOne of the most significant events of Raymond III's reign was the Battle of Harim in 1164 He led the combined forces of the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi the ruler of the Zengid Dynasty Raymond III's forces were defeated and he was captured by Nur ad-Din He remained a prisoner for the next seven years until he was released in 1171\n\nAfter his release Raymond III continued to play an active role in the politics of the Crusader states He supported the marriage of Sibylla of Jerusalem to Guy of Lusignan in 1180 and was present at the coronation of their son Baldwin V as the King of Jerusalem in 1183\n\nRaymond III's final years were marked by the approach of the Ayyubid Dynasty led by Saladin He joined the forces of the Crusader states at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 but was once again defeated by Saladin He was taken prisoner and was executed by Saladin's troops shortly after the 